{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 makes an explicit barrier/suspend event a necessary condition ('if and only if') for safe resumption, but append-only identity is preserved whenever the last recorded event was fully persisted before exhaustion, even if no barrier/suspend marker was emitted. A run that exhausts the budget cleanly after a completed event has a complete, uncorrupted event log; continuing with the same causal fingerprint from that committed boundary would not inject a new identity. By refusing all such cases, the target over-constrains the permissible-resumption set and fails to determine exactly when resumption may occur.","counterexample":["Run exhausts budget immediately after a fully persisted event E; no barrier/suspend marker was emitted; the causal fingerprint at resumption matches the run start."],"target":"032d4125e6b7718672a0ccaeb0b97a669e9f311755b54dee0b235210851451d5"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's categorical claim that resumption must always be refused is inconsistent with its own acknowledged countercondition: if the system emits a 'budget exhausted' marker as part of the logical event stream, the environmental interrupt becomes a logical event, so continuation from that marker with a matching causal fingerprint can preserve append-only identity. The target asserts always-refuse rather than adjudicating this rival criterion, so it does not determine exactly when resumption may occur.","counterexample":["System emits a logical budget_exhausted event before truncation; at resumption the program/input/checkpoint fingerprint matches that marker."],"target":"b0a3d571038c9314c15157a120d764f0c6da706958888a3f119a9a2d2f7c49df"}]}